interior.complex-factorisation-location-.formal:
Write $\mathfrak{M} := -M\,(r - Ia\cos\theta)^{-3}$ for the scale in Fact \claimref{interior.imported-kerr-curvature}, so that $R(B) = -\tfrac{\mathfrak M}{2}(B + 3\sigma_rB\sigma_r) = \mathfrak{M}\,S(B)$ with $S = 1 - 3\Pi$ the shape operator of Proposition \claimref{interior.exact-scale-decoupling} built on the principal plane pair. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the subalgebra $\R \oplus \R I \subset \Cl(1,3)$ is a field isomorphic to $\C$: $I^2 = -1$, and $z = x + Iy$ is invertible in it if and only if $z\tilde z = x^2 + y^2 \neq 0$, where $\tilde z := x - Iy$;
\item the Kerr curvature is the Schwarzschild curvature with $r^3$ replaced by $z^3 = (r - Ia\cos\theta)^3$ in the scale, $R = \mathfrak M\,S$ against Schwarzschild's $R = -(M/r^3)S$: same shape $S$, same identity $S^2 + S = 2$, same spectrum ratio $2{:}(-1)$, now with eigenvalues valued in $\C$;
\item for $a \neq 0$ the non-invertibility locus of the scale's base $z = r - Ia\cos\theta$ within the subalgebra is exactly $\{r^2 + a^2\cos^2\theta = 0\} = \{r = 0,\ \theta = \pi/2\}$: the ring. In particular the disc interior $\{r = 0,\ \theta \neq \pi/2\}$ carries an invertible scale and finite curvature: the framework's own criterion, invertibility within the algebra, locates the Kerr singularity correctly and accounts for the regularity of the disc without further input;
\item at $a = 0$ the factorisation reduces identically to Proposition \claimref{interior.exact-scale-decoupling}.
\end{enumerate}

interior.exact-parallelism-convergence-r.formal:
For the Schwarzschild interior field $(\hb, \Omega)$ on flat spacetime in the synchronous gauge of Lemma \claimref{interior.kasner-form-interior}, with physical frame $g_\mu = \hb^{-1}(e_\mu)$ (Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}), let $e_0 := g_\tau$ and let $(e_1, e_2, e_3)$ be the orthonormal frame obtained by normalising the physical vectors $(g_t, g_\theta, g_\varphi)$ (so $e_0, \ldots, e_3$ name this normalised physical frame, and $e_\mu$ with a label index $\mu \in \{\tau, t, \theta, \varphi\}$ the background vectors). Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the frame is \emph{exactly} $D$-parallel along the free-fall worldlines of velocity $g_\tau$ (the geodesics of the derived metric), at every $\tau > 0$: parallel transport into the singularity is not an asymptotic question for the frame;
\item with $\sigma_1 := \partial_\tau \log \sqrt{A}$ and $\sigma_2 = \sigma_3 := \partial_\tau \log r$ the Hubble-normalised expansion rates, in units $2M = 1$,
\[
\tau\sigma_1 \;=\; -\tfrac13 - \tfrac{4}{15}R + O(R^2),
\qquad
\tau\sigma_2 \;=\; \tau\sigma_3 \;=\; \tfrac23 - \tfrac{2}{15}R + O(R^2),
\]
so the rates converge to the Kasner exponents at the rate $O(\tau^{2/3})$, with explicit leading constants;
\item every relative deviation of the interior field, read through its derived metric, from the exact Kasner model with these exponents (scale factors, the diagonal expansion rates $\sigma_i$, and the curvature of the $S^2$ sections measured against the dominant scale $\tau^{-2}$) is $O(\tau^{2/3})$: the approach is velocity-dominated with one uniform power-law rate, each deviation one-signed near the singularity, with no oscillations; the sphere's rotation coefficient lies outside the Kasner comparison and carries the separate Hubble-normalised rate $O(\tau^{1/3})$ (Lemma \claimref{interior.interior-connection-exactly}, Definition \claimref{obj.interior.weighted-blow-up-hubble-normalisation-admissible}).
\end{enumerate}

interior.unipotency-across-kerr-family.formal:
Let $P_k(a) := (a\cdot k)\,k$ with $Q(k) = 0$. Then, exactly:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $P_k^2 = 0$, so $\{1 + sHP_k\}$ is again a one-parameter unipotent family and $\hb^{-1} = 1 - H P_k$ in closed form wherever the gauge field is defined;
\item $\hb(a)\cdot\hb(b) = a\cdot b + 2H\,(a\cdot k)(b\cdot k)$ with no higher corrections, and equally $\hb^{-1}(a)\cdot\hb^{-1}(b) = a\cdot b - 2H\,(a\cdot k)(b\cdot k)$; since the metric of the section “Setting and conventions” is $g(a,b) = \hb^{-1}(a)\cdot\hb^{-1}(b)$, the gauge field reproduces the Kerr-Schild metric $g = \eta - 2H\,k\otimes k$ exactly, in the $(+,-,-,-)$ signature, the first display being the inverse metric;
\item the entire Kerr family, Schwarzschild included, is therefore a unipotent deformation of the identity, and the deformation is now built on a \emph{null} vector: the shear of Proposition \claimref{interior.gauge-trajectory-unipotent-ray}, whose kernel direction was spacelike-orthogonal at finite radius and became null only in the limit, is literally null for rotation, at every point.
\end{enumerate}

interior.weighted-limit-theorem.formal:
For the Schwarzschild interior, and more generally for every admissible metric in the sense of Definition \claimref{obj.interior.weighted-blow-up-hubble-normalisation-admissible}, each read as the derived metric of a gravitational field $(\hb, \Omega)$ on flat spacetime (Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}; the Levi-Civita connection being its rotation-gauge field $\Omega$ on unit physical directions, in the sign of that Definition, in which the Cartan forms of a Kasner field assemble to minus the weighted boosts, and the tractor connection its $\Cl(2,4)$ gauge field of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.gtg-tractor-gauge-field-reproduces-tractor-connection}):
\begin{enumerate}
\item the Hubble-normalised Levi-Civita and tractor connections, evaluated on unit tangential directions, extend continuously to the exceptional boundary; for Schwarzschild the correction is $O(\tau^{2/3})$ in the boost coefficients and $O(\tau^{1/3})$ in the rotation coefficients;
\item the parallel scale tractor $I_g$ of Theorems \claimref{interior.covariant-form-vacuum-equations} and \claimref{interior.scale-tractor-leaves-cone} extends to a parallel section of the boundary bundle of Definition \claimref{obj.interior.weighted-blow-up-hubble-normalisation-admissible}: in the parallel trivialisation it is the constant section, and every limiting connection value annihilates it;
\item the limiting tangential connection is
\[
\widehat\Omega_0 \;=\; -\sum_{i=1}^3 p_i\, B_i \otimes \hat\theta^i ,
\]
the three boosts of the adapted frame weighted by minus the Kasner exponents, while every tractor coupling (the $\sigma$- and $\rho$-slot interchange terms of \claimref{interior.imported-tractor-connection}) and every rotation coefficient scales to zero;
\item consequently the limiting connection values lie in $\Lambda^2 W$, and the boundary holonomy algebra of Definition \claimref{obj.interior.weighted-blow-up-hubble-normalisation-admissible} is exactly $\Lambda^2 W \cong \mathfrak{so}(1,3)$: the two nilpotent summands $\nInf \wedge W$, $\nO \wedge W$ and the grading element $\nInf \wedge \nO$ receive no limiting value. For $\Lambda = 0$ the frozen point is null and this algebra is the Levi factor of its parabolic stabiliser (Proposition \claimref{interior.stabilisers-flat-model}), with no component in the shearing nilradical; for $\Lambda \neq 0$ the frozen point is non-null, its stabiliser is reductive with no nilradical (Proposition \claimref{interior.non-null-stabilisers-section-they-act}), and $\Lambda^2 W$ lies inside that stabiliser as a proper subalgebra (Theorem \claimref{interior.schwarzschild-de-sitter-weighted-limit}).
\end{enumerate}
For Schwarzschild the convergence holds at transport level along the abelian directions, exactly rather than by estimate, in two separate statements. Along the $t$- and $\theta$-coordinate lines the connection is a fixed bivector times a scalar, so the interior and Kasner-model transports commute, and their Hubble-normalised generators on the unit direction differ by $\tau\sigma_i - p_i = O(\tau^{2/3})$: over segments of fixed Hubble-normalised length the two transports differ by a boost of angle $O(\tau^{2/3}) \to 0$, with no Gronwall argument (over a fixed \emph{coordinate} segment the transports individually diverge, and no claim is made about their comparison). Along the $\tau$-geodesics the coordinate frame evolves diagonally, and its log-ratio against the Kasner model is $\int_0^\tau (\sigma_i - p_i/s)\,ds = O(\tau^{2/3}) \to 0$, one scale factor at a time. Along the $\varphi$-lines the connection on the coordinate vector carries the $\tau$-independent rotation $\cos\theta\,R_{23}$ beside a divergent boost, the two do not commute, and this theorem makes no transport-level claim there: the comparison over segments of fixed Hubble-normalised length, along the $\varphi$-lines and along every tangential curve of every admissible metric, is Theorem \claimref{interior.transport-level-convergence-along-tangential-curves} with Corollary \claimref{interior.schwarzschild-lines-exactly}; what remains open in general is the tangential regularity of the boundary data, the control of tangential derivatives of $E_\tau$, registered as a successor problem in the section “Scope, and successor problems”.

synthesis.bkl-bounce-invariant-holonomy-.formal:
Let the gravitational field $(\hb, \Omega)$ on the collar of flat spacetime (Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}) be Einstein, $\mathcal{R}(a) = \Lambda a$ in the store's sign (so its derived metric has $\Ric = \Lambda g$), in synchronous gauge, with Hubble-normalised rotation-gauge field $\widehat\Omega_{\tau}$ on unit tangential directions as in Definition \claimref{obj.interior.weighted-blow-up-hubble-normalisation-admissible}, but with no fixed Kasner data. Parametrise the Kasner circle $\sum_{i}p_{i} = \sum_{i}p_{i}^{2} = 1$ by the BKL parameter, $p(u) = \bigl(-u,\ 1 + u,\ u(1 + u)\bigr)/(1 + u + u^{2})$, $u \ge 1$, up to the order of the axes. For a Kasner point $p$ and a spatial rotation $R$ of the orthonormal physical frame (a rotation-gauge transformation), the Kasner model in the rotated frame $Re_{i}$ is $-\sum_{i}p_{i}\,(RB_{i}R^{-1})\otimes(R\hat\theta)^{i}$, with $(R\hat\theta)^{i}$ the coframe dual to $Re_{i}$ (the sign of the Definition's $\Omega$, as in Definition \claimref{obj.interior.weighted-blow-up-hubble-normalisation-admissible}).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \emph{(Era ends.)} On the arc $1 \le u \le 2$, $\min_{i}|p_{i}(u)| \ge \tfrac27$, with equality only at $u = 2$. Every era of the BKL map ends in an epoch on this arc.
\item[(ii)] \emph{(The transport bound is bounce-invariant.)} At every time $\tau$, for every Kasner point $p$ and every rotation $R$, put $E_{\tau} := \widehat\Omega_{\tau} + \sum_{i}p_{i}\,(RB_{i}R^{-1})\otimes(R\hat\theta)^{i}$. For every tangential curve at time $\tau$ inside a compact $K$ of Hubble-normalised length $\lambda$, the $D$-transport and the transport of that Kasner model satisfy $\|T_{\gamma}(\lambda) - T^{0}_{\gamma}(\lambda)\| \le \lambda\|E_{\tau}\|_{K}\,e^{\lambda(1 + \|E_{\tau}\|_{K})}$, the bound of Theorem \claimref{interior.transport-level-convergence-along-tangential-curves} with the same constant whichever point of the Kasner circle, and hence whichever epoch or era, and whichever frame the model belongs to.
\item[(iii)] \emph{(The holonomy algebra is bounce-invariant.)} Suppose there are times $\tau_{k} \to 0$, Kasner points $p^{(k)} = p(u_{k})$ with $1 \le u_{k} \le 2$, and rotations $R_{k}$ such that $\|E_{\tau_{k}}\|_{K} \to 0$ for these models on every compact $K$. Then the accumulation values of $\widehat\Omega_{\tau}$ on unit tangential directions as $\tau \to 0$ include the three weighted boosts $-p_{i}\,RB_{i}R^{-1}$, $i = 1, 2, 3$, for some $p = p(u)$ with $1 \le u \le 2$ and some rotation $R$, and the Lie algebra generated by all accumulation values is exactly $\Lambda^{2}W \cong \mathfrak{so}(1,3)$. So clause (4) of Theorem \claimref{interior.weighted-limit-theorem} holds with limiting values replaced by accumulation values.
\end{enumerate}

synthesis.bkl-bounces-no-framewise-invar.formal:
Let a Kasner epoch be a triple $p = (p_{1}, p_{2}, p_{3})$ on the constraint variety $\sum p_{i} = 1$, $\sum p_{i}^{2} = 1$, carried by the axes of a fixed frame, with limiting tangential connection $\widehat\Omega_{0}(p) = -\sum_{i} p_{i} B_{i}\otimes\hat\theta^{i}$ (the sign of the rotation-gauge field of Definition \claimref{obj.interior.weighted-blow-up-hubble-normalisation-admissible}) as in Theorem \claimref{interior.weighted-limit-theorem}. Take as the model of a bounce the BKL transition law: if $p_{a} < 0$, then $p'_{a} = -p_{a}/(1 + 2p_{a})$ and $p'_{b} = (p_{b} + 2p_{a})/(1 + 2p_{a})$ for $b \neq a$; and write the ordered triple as $p(u) = (-u,\ 1 + u,\ u(1 + u))/(1 + u + u^{2})$, $u \geq 1$. The bounce lemma (the law preserves both Kasner relations and acts as $u \mapsto u - 1$, followed by $u \mapsto 1/(u - 1)$ at the end of an era, each era ending in an epoch with $1 \leq u \leq 2$) is filed alongside, synthesis.bkl-bounce-invariant-holonomy-and-bound, and is recalled in the proof; on $p(u)$ the bouncing axis receives $p_{2}(u - 1) > 0$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \emph{(No framewise limit, if the approach follows the BKL law.)} Suppose the approach to the singularity passes through the sequence of epochs that the transition law generates. At a bounce from an epoch with $1 \leq u \leq 2$ the coefficient of the fixed boost $B_{a}$ in $\widehat\Omega_{0}$ jumps by $u/(u^{2} + u + 1) + u/(u^{2} - u + 1) \geq 20/21$. Every era (maximal run of steps $u \mapsto u - 1$) ends with such an epoch, and for irrational initial $u$ there are infinitely many eras, so the fixed-frame values $\widehat\Omega_{0}(p^{(n)})$ along the bounce sequence are not Cauchy.
\item[(ii)] \emph{(Rational orbits.)} For rational $u = [a_{0}; a_{1}, \ldots, a_{k}]$ the orbit reaches $u = 1$, the Schwarzschild exponents $(-\tfrac13, \tfrac23, \tfrac23)$ of Lemma \claimref{interior.kasner-form-interior}, after $a_{0} + \cdots + a_{k} - 1$ bounces, and the next bounce sends them to $(1, 0, 0)$, where no exponent is negative and the orbit stops. These are the two collision points of Proposition \claimref{sheaf.collision-census}. At $(1, 0, 0)$ the triple is outside the admissible class of Definition \claimref{obj.interior.weighted-blow-up-hubble-normalisation-admissible} ($p_{i} \neq 0$ fails) and the limiting values generate the one-dimensional algebra $\R B_{a}$ (the Taub point, flat Kasner; also recorded alongside), against $\Lambda^{2}W \cong \mathfrak{so}(1,3)$ at every other epoch, where all three exponents are nonzero.
\item[(iii)] \emph{(No continuous invariant; standard.)} Every continuous function of the unordered triple that is invariant under the transition law is constant. In particular $e_{3} = p_{1}p_{2}p_{3}$, the coordinate of the unordered triple ($e_{1} = 1$, $e_{2} = 0$ being fixed) and, times eight, the coefficient of the wall counterterm of Theorem \claimref{dark-energy.wall-counterterm}, changes at every bounce except one from the epoch $u = (1 + \sqrt5)/2$, the fixed point of the map. The absence of nonconstant continuous invariants is classical for the Gauss-map dynamics of the BKL parameter and is transport-absent; the content specific to the series is its consequence for the limiting connection and for the wall counterterm.
\end{enumerate}
